Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 12:48:22 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: LOGIC: Superrationality (PROOF)

Miron Cuperman (in message #93-9-933) gives a very nice probabilistic argument
which proves that

>... the best strategy for a super-rational entity when
>playing the prisoners' dilemma is to cooperate. ...

...and knowing that, of course, the best strategy is to defect.

This does not arise because you have applied a probabilistic argument to a
problem whose `solution' is applied at a single moment of time, and is not
due to the particular parameter choices.  It arises simply because any proof
that would result in a best strategy of "cooperate" would be an incentive to
do just the opposite at any iteration (assuming that D>C).  This is true even
if the game goes on indefinitely.

The iterated problem demands a more sophisticated interaction between the
players - they need to react to each other's actions.  This is why strategies
such as tit-for-tat have been so successful in evolutionary simulations.  Any
strategies that respond consistently as either "cooperate" or "defect" are
quickly weeded out (although no single player can successfully invade a
population of consistent defectors... unusual initial conditions).  A strategy
for playing the prisoner's dilemma is dependent upon the nature of the
population from which your opponent is chosen, and this population does not
stabilize at a homogeneous distribution of any strategy of consistency.
Sophisticated interactions are necessary among any system of agents with
conflicting interests - this is something superrationality ignores.

Paul

(The net-game CoreWar is an interesting example of a fluctuating population of
strategies.  Anyone know if it has converged to a solution?)



