From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: DC-X Flies

In reply to Derek on the use of fission power for a launcher.

There is a launch concept called a 'nuclear gas gun' that does this.
You drive hydrogen gas through a fission reactor core to heat it up,
then use the hot hydrogen to accelerate a projectile.  You essentially
have the exhaust of a nuclear rocket connected to a gun barrel.

But this is an expensive way to get the result on the Earth's
surface.  What you want for a gas gun is a large amount of hot hydrogen
generated quickly.  You want hot gas because the gun muzzle
velocity is dependant on the average speed of the molecules in
the gas.  You want hydrogen for the same reason - it is the lightest
molecule.  You want to generate it quickly because it is hard to
store.

The least expensive way to do this I am aware of is to use a particle
bed heat exchanger.  This is a chamber filled with aluminum oxide
grains about 1/2 millimeter in size.  Aluminum oxide is cheap (about
$1/lb in bulk), and can be heated to high temperatures.  You heat
the bed from the center using any convenient heat source.  The
outer edge, near the container walls, stays relatively cool, so you
can use ordinary steel for the pressure vessel.  Run your hydrogen
through the bed and heat it to 2000 degrees in under a second.

If you want to launch many small projectiles for getting things into
space, this is the least expensive approach I am aware of.

Dani Eder

