Re: Mag-Lev & Ramjets

The highest speed for a fairly large mag-lev system is about 350mph
(as far as I know) for an experimental system in Japan.  Existing
ramjets run up to about Mach 3-4 (Mach 1 = 300 meters/sec), and the
upper bound for useful ramjets is around Mach 8, but I am not
aware of any flying ramjets at that speed.  Orbital speed is Mach 25
or so.  The whole point of the NASP program was to develop a
'scramjet', or supersonic combustion ramjet, that in theory could
go up to Mach 25, although in practice would probably become less
efficient than a rocket around Mach 12-15.  You need laser power
(or any type of engine power) of around 100kW per kg to get things
into orbit.  If you have less than this, you hit the ground before 
you get to orbital speed.  The biggest lasers built on an experimental
basis are on the order of a few MW, so you can't get much payload
into orbit this way (and those are finicky experimental lasers).

So these approaches (mag-lev, ramjet/scramjet, & laser) all require
lots of development work to be very useful for space launch.

Dani Eder

