Dani Eder writes:

> I don't share the urgency of Mr. Martin in buying high-power weapons.
> Having observed the advances in the computer-controlled machine-tool
> industry, it will shortly be possible for hobbyists to make guns in
> their garage fairly easily, with less skill than the hobbyists who
> make guns now.  There are already desktop milling machines available
> that are controlled by a PC and cost under $10,000.
> 
> An effective gun ban would require the elimination of machine tools and
> computers as well, for with these, you can 'roll your own' weapons.

A fascinating future prospect, but let me list some possible attacks.
Don't know if they'll succeed, but be aware of them:

1. A guy was busted for building a gun that fires from what looks to
be a pager or beeper. When he tried to market the design to gun
manufacturers, the Feds swooped in (seems one of the gun makers narced
him, the better to control competition, I suspect). He's under
indictment, says CNN, and faces several years in the klink, for
illegally building weapons (or somesuch...I don't know the exact name
of the statute).

Building a gun in private is a different matter, of course, but keep
this in mind.

2. Civil and criminal forfeiture. Those with assets (cf. Duncan's
posts about becoming "judgement proof") face dire consequences when
illegal acts--like making or owning a gun the State says you cannot
have--are discovered. This has no effect on the street punk, or the
Mafia wiseguy, but it has a chilling and "pacifying" effect on most
others.

(Whit Diffie's point about how the forfeiture laws have effectively
driven dope smoking out of middle class homes, and how this may be
used to implemetn restrictions on crypto: use a cipher, lose your
business, your car, your computers, and your house.)

3. Target practice. More and more land is being restricted for
shooting, even if it's privately owned and far away from highways,
schools, etc. Shooting ranges are getting nosier and nosier about what
they look for (I suspect the day is coming when target ranges will be
effectively "deputized" to aid in the hunt for illegal weapons.
Ironically, they'll only get law-abiding folks---but that's the effect
of a lot of gun laws.)

One range, in Milpitas, CA, got very nosy about my HK SP-89 (newly
added to the import ban list). After asking what I "needed" such a gun
for, and persisting even when I shrugged, I decided to tell him the
literal truth. "I'm an anarchist," I told him. He let me go out to my
position and said no more about anything. I doubt he reported me (to
whom?). Too much paperwork. For now.

4. Distribution. Any system of basement machine shops that cannot get
weapons distributed will be a marginal contributor to future access to
guns. Just not enough of these machine shops, barring dramatic
advances in NC tools.

....

So, I don't see home machine shops as a viable answer to the disarming
of Americans. A useful emergency matter, though.

Frankly, the 100 million or more existing guns in America will remain
a resource that can be "mined" for many years even after a ban. These
existing guns, most of them unregistered and untraceable (unless
hunting lodges and target ranges start recording serial numbers
soon....and this wouldn't surprise me one bit), will circulate in a
black market for many years to come.

But we need all the sources we can get.

-Tim May




